@page "/privacy"
@layout PublicLayout

<PageTitle>Privacy - YahtzeeSim</PageTitle>

<article class="doc-page">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>YahtzeeSim is a personal Yahtzee scorecard app. This page explains what it stores.</p>

    <h2>Signing in</h2>
    <p>
        You sign in with Google. Google tells us only a private, app-specific user ID that proves who you are.
        We do not receive or store your email address, name or photo, and we never see your Google password.
    </p>

    <h2>What we store</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>That ID, so we can recognise you when you come back.</li>
        <li>The nickname you choose. It is shown to other players on the leaderboard.</li>
        <li>Your finished-game scores and when they were played.</li>
        <li>
            A log of the moves you make in each game, for gameplay research: every roll (with the dice that came up and
            which dice you kept), every hold or release of a die, every box you score or cross out (with the dice and
            points), every undo, and the time of each. It is tied to your account like your scores, and is not shown to other players.
            You can see the play-style story built from it under Account, My play style; it is worked out on your device
            from your last 10 finished Full Sheet games.
        </li>
        <li>Your play-style badge (for example Yahtzee Hunter), worked out on your device from those games. It describes how you play, not how well.</li>
        <li>
            Challenges you start or join: the challenge name, who is in it, when it ends and the score of the game you played for it.
            Only the players in a challenge can see it.
        </li>
        <li>
            Groups you create, join or ask to join: the group name, whether you are an admin, and your request while it waits for approval.
            The members of a group can see each other's nicknames; join requests are only shown to the group's admins.
        </li>
        <li>
            If you turn on notifications on a device: the push address your browser gives us for that device and its encryption keys,
            so we can send you challenge news. Notifications go through your browser's push service (Apple, Google or Mozilla),
            encrypted so the service cannot read them. Turning notifications off removes the address.
        </li>
        <li>A sign-in cookie, so you stay signed in for about 30 days.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>On your device the app also keeps your game in progress, pen colour and settings.</p>

    <h2>Sharing</h2>
    <p>
        Your nickname, best scores and play-style badge are visible to other players, and signed-in players can find your nickname
        to invite you to a challenge. The names of groups and how many members they have can be found by every signed-in player so they can ask to join;
        who is in a group is only shown to its members. Anyone in a group can invite the whole group to a challenge. A challenge and its results are only shown to the players in it (a challenge game also counts
        on the leaderboard like any other game). We do not sell or share
        your data and we use no advertising or tracking.
    </p>

    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>To have your account and scores deleted, contact the person who runs this site and give them your nickname.</p>

    <p><a href="">Back to the game</a></p>
</article>
